    "q-100612": {
      "dom_id": "step-q100612",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "If $\\frac{{log}_{3}⁡(x+24)}{3}=2$, then $x=$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we rearrange our equation to isolate the logarithmic term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{log}_{3}⁡(x+24)}{3} & =2 \\\\ {log}_{3}⁡(x+24) & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $3,$ so we use this base for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{3}⁡(x+24) & =6 \\\\ {3}^{{log}_{3}⁡(x+24)} & ={3}^{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${3}^{{log}_{3}⁡(x+24)}=x+24.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+24 & ={3}^{6} \\\\ x+24 & =729 \\\\ x & =705.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
