    "q-110771": {
      "dom_id": "step-q110771",
      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "If $4{log}_{9}⁡x-2{log}_{9}⁡x=1,$ then what is $x?$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we rearrange our equation to isolate the logarithmic term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{log}_{9}⁡x-2{log}_{9}⁡x & =1 \\\\ (4-2){log}_{9}⁡x & =1 \\\\ 2{log}_{9}⁡x & =1 \\\\ {log}_{9}⁡x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $9,$ so we use this base for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{9}⁡x & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {9}^{{log}_{9}⁡x} & ={9}^{1/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${9}^{{log}_{9}⁡x}=x.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={9}^{1/2} \\\\ x & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\ x & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
